# redteam-regen — weekly regeneration of the generative red-team set (Plan Phase
# 2/4, Analysis T-A).
#
# TEMPLATE for the TARGET MCP-server repo (e.g. zurich-opendata-mcp), NOT the
# auditor. Ships as `.yml.template` so GitHub does not run it in the auditor repo.
# To activate: copy to `.github/workflows/redteam-regen.yml`, and copy the two
# scripts it drives — `scripts/regen_guard.py` and `scripts/drift_issue.py`.
# Both are stdlib-only, so nothing else needs installing for them.
#
# It runs `promptfoo redteam generate` (pinned attacker model) and, if the
# committed `redteam.generated.yaml` changed, opens a PR. Regeneration is thus a
# DELIBERATE, human-reviewed change — the eval gate always runs against COMMITTED
# cases, never a moving target (T-A). The eval of those cases is the graded CI job.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS FILE IS SHAPED AROUND
# --------------------------------------
# This workflow runs on `schedule` and nothing else, and until now it had no
# `issues: write` and no routing. That combination has no addressee: a red weekly
# run appears in no pull request, and the job summary is seen by whoever opens
# the run, which for a cron is nobody. It is not a hypothetical — a scheduled
# guard elsewhere in this portfolio was red across six consecutive merges and was
# never answered.
#
# Worse than a red run is a quiet one. When regeneration stops, the graded CI job
# keeps evaluating the cases that are already committed and stays green; the
# adversarial set simply stops growing, and nothing anywhere turns a colour. So
# the finding needs a destination that survives: a tracking issue that is opened
# or updated on a bad week and CLOSES ITSELF on a good one. Without the close it
# becomes standing noise, and the answer to noise is to switch the guard off —
# the close is what decides whether this job is still running in six months.
#
# WHY THE DECISION IS NOT IN THIS FILE
# ------------------------------------
# `echo` and a tool call may live in YAML; an `if` that decides red or green may
# not (`OPS-008`). Inline logic runs only inside a workflow, so it cannot be
# unit-tested, and what cannot be unit-tested cannot be mutation-tested. That is
# not a style preference: `scripts/regen_guard.py` has a third state precisely
# because moving the decision out of YAML made a two-state collapse visible, the
# same way it did for `live-probe.yml.template`.

name: redteam-regen

on:
  schedule:
    # Thursdays 05:23 UTC. Odd minute to dodge the top-of-hour scheduler stampede.
    - cron: "23 5 * * 4"
  workflow_dispatch: {}

# Least privilege: read code, write a branch + open the PR, and file the tracking
# issue that gives a scheduled run somebody to report to.
permissions:
  contents: write
  pull-requests: write
  issues: write

concurrency:
  group: redteam-regen
  cancel-in-progress: false

jobs:
  regen:
    name: regenerate red-team set
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    env:
      PROMPTFOO_VERSION: "0.121.17"
      # Attacker/synthesiser model — a resolvable, non-writer-family model. Pinned
      # so a bump is a reviewable change. Its key comes from secrets.
      REDTEAM_ATTACKER: openai:gpt-4o-mini
      OPENAI_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.OPENAI_API_KEY }}
      REDTEAM_SET: promptfoo/redteam/redteam.generated.yaml
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: "20"
      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.11"

      # `continue-on-error` on both working steps is what makes the routing step
      # reachable at all. Without it a failed regeneration ends the job, and the
      # only state this guard could ever report would be the one it already
      # reports badly: nothing.
      - name: Regenerate committed red-team cases
        id: generate
        continue-on-error: true
        run: bash promptfoo/redteam/generate.sh

      - name: Open a PR if the red-team set changed
        id: pr
        continue-on-error: true
        uses: peter-evans/create-pull-request@v6
        with:
          add-paths: promptfoo/redteam/redteam.generated.yaml
          branch: redteam/regen
          title: "redteam: regenerate committed adversarial cases"
          commit-message: "redteam: regenerate committed adversarial cases (weekly)"
          body: |
            Automated weekly `promptfoo redteam generate` run.

            The committed red-team set changed. Review the new/updated adversarial
            cases in `promptfoo/redteam/redteam.generated.yaml` before merging — the
            graded CI job evaluates these committed cases, so this is the reviewed
            gate for expanding red-team coverage (Analysis T-A).
          labels: redteam
          draft: true

      # Classify the run: finding / clear / unknown. Three, not two — "no finding
      # was produced" and "everything is in order" are different sentences, and
      # only the second may close an issue.
      #
      # `unknown` is the state a TEMPLATE needs most. Most repositories that copy
      # this file will not have configured an attacker key on day one. Folding
      # that into `finding` opens a ticket in every one of them for a workflow
      # nobody switched on; folding it into `clear` closes a real ticket on a run
      # that generated nothing. Both end with the guard switched off.
      #
      # Step outcomes and the secret's presence go through the environment, not
      # through `${{ }}` interpolated into a shell command line.
      - name: Classify the regeneration run
        id: guard
        if: always()
        env:
          HAVE_KEY: ${{ secrets.OPENAI_API_KEY != '' }}
          GENERATE_OUTCOME: ${{ steps.generate.outcome }}
          PR_OUTCOME: ${{ steps.pr.outcome }}
        run: |
          python scripts/regen_guard.py \
            --have-key "${HAVE_KEY}" \
            --generate-outcome "${GENERATE_OUTCOME}" \
            --pr-outcome "${PR_OUTCOME}" \
            --artifact "${REDTEAM_SET}" \
            --out redteam-regen-report.md

      # Route it to the one addressee a scheduled job has. The router is the same
      # tested three-state script `live-probe.yml.template` uses; it is given this
      # run as a single probe, and it reads an ABSENT `alert=` as "did not run" by
      # its own rule rather than by a second copy of that rule here. On `unknown`
      # the classifier writes neither the verdict nor the report, so both halves
      # have to agree before anything is opened or closed.
      #
      # `always()` is load-bearing: `clear` and `unknown` only exist if this step
      # runs on a good week and on a broken one.
      - name: Route the regeneration tracking issue (open / update / close)
        if: always()
        env:
          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          GUARD_ALERT: ${{ steps.guard.outputs.alert }}
          GUARD_OUTCOME: ${{ steps.guard.outcome }}
        run: |
          python scripts/drift_issue.py \
            --marker redteam-regen-stale \
            --label redteam \
            --label-color b60205 \
            --title "[redteam-regen] The committed adversarial set is no longer being regenerated" \
            --body-file redteam-regen-report.md \
            --probe "redteam-regen:${GUARD_OUTCOME}:${GUARD_ALERT}:redteam-regen-report.md"

      # Deliberately no final "exit 1 if there was a finding" step, unlike the
      # equivalent guard in mcp-audit-skill. That one also runs on `push` to main,
      # where a red run is at least visible to whoever merged; this one runs on
      # `schedule` only, so a red job is the exact non-event the issue exists to
      # replace. Same convention as `live-probe.yml.template`.

      - name: Upload the report
        if: always()
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: redteam-regen-report
          path: redteam-regen-report.md
          if-no-files-found: ignore
